⚠️ **IMPORTANT: ADO question subtasks get the question label, Jira ones do not**

The new ADO path labels each created question:

```javascript
// createAdoQuestionTicket (line 57)
var result = tracker.createTicketWithParent(projectKey, adoType, summary, description, parentKey, {
    labels: [questionLabel]
});
```

…but the Jira path (`buildJiraQuestionFields`, this hunk) builds `fieldsJson` with `summary`/`description`/`issuetype`/`parent`/`priority` and **no `labels` field**, so question subtasks created on Jira carry no question label. The canonical `js/createQuestionsAndAssignForReview.js` includes `labels: [questionLabel]` in the Jira `fieldsJson` as well — so the two copies of the workflow disagree, and any downstream filtering of question subtasks by label behaves differently per provider (the label is genuinely used by the new ADO branch, so the Jira omission is now visible).

Old root behavior is preserved for Jira (legacy code also sent no labels), so this is not a regression — but since the PR routes both providers through one abstraction, aligning the Jira branch with the js/ script removes the asymmetry:

```javascript
function buildJiraQuestionFields(summary, description, parentKey, priority, questionLabel) {
    var fieldsJson = {
        summary: summary,
        description: description,
        issuetype: { name: 'Subtask' },
        parent: { key: parentKey },
        labels: [questionLabel]
    };
    ...
}
```

💡 **SUGGESTION: First and last branches of `updateRestoredDescription` are identical**

```javascript
function updateRestoredDescription(ticketKey, mergedDesc, tracker) {
    if (typeof jira_update_description === 'function') {
        return tracker.updateDescription(ticketKey, mergedDesc);   // branch 1
    }
    if (typeof jira_update_ticket === 'function') {
        return jira_update_ticket(ticketKey, { fields: { description: mergedDesc } });
    }
    return tracker.updateDescription(ticketKey, mergedDesc);       // branch 3 — same as branch 1
}
```

Since the provider is guaranteed `'jira'` at this call site, `tracker.updateDescription` *is* `jira_update_description` — branches 1 and 3 make the same call (branch 3 just lets a missing tool surface as a `ReferenceError`). The whole decision collapses to a single legacy-tool guard:

```javascript
function updateRestoredDescription(ticketKey, mergedDesc, tracker) {
    if (typeof jira_update_description !== 'function' && typeof jira_update_ticket === 'function') {
        return jira_update_ticket(ticketKey, { fields: { description: mergedDesc } });
    }
    return tracker.updateDescription(ticketKey, mergedDesc);
}
```

Same behavior, one less branch to test (the existing "legacy fallback" test would still pass).

