💡 **SUGGESTION: `'question'` fallback disagrees with the canonical default `'q'`**

```javascript
function resolveQuestionLabel(projectConfig) {
    return (projectConfig && projectConfig.labels && projectConfig.labels.QUESTION) || 'question';
}
```

`js/config.js:59` ships `LABELS.QUESTION: 'q'` — that is the value every config-defaulted flow uses when no project override exists. When `projectConfig` fails to load (configLoader absent or broken — exactly the case this fallback covers), ADO questions would be labelled `'question'` while the rest of the system expects `'q'`.

Falling back to the shared constant instead of a literal keeps the two layers consistent:

```javascript
var configModule = require('./js/config.js');

function resolveQuestionLabel(projectConfig) {
    return (projectConfig && projectConfig.labels && projectConfig.labels.QUESTION) ||
        configModule.LABELS.QUESTION;
}
```

Minor, only reachable on the ADO path with a failed config load — hence a suggestion.

💡 **SUGGESTION: `ticketKey` is interpolated into the REST path unencoded**

```javascript
function buildChangelogUrl(ticketKey, projectConfig) {
    return resolveJiraBaseUrl(projectConfig) + '/rest/api/3/issue/' + ticketKey + '/changelog?maxResults=100';
}
```

The key comes from `params.ticketKey || params.ticket.key` — orchestrator-controlled today (and the interpolation matches the legacy code), so this is hardening rather than a fix. A malformed key (spaces, `?`, `/`) would silently corrupt the request path instead of failing clearly. Cheap defensive options:

```javascript
var encoded = encodeURIComponent(String(ticketKey));
return resolveJiraBaseUrl(projectConfig) + '/rest/api/3/issue/' + encoded + '/changelog?maxResults=100';
```

or a key-shape guard via the shared `js/common/ticketKeyShapes.js` helper. No secrets are involved either way — the URL only ever reaches the configured Jira host through `jira_execute_request`.

